views {

  view gym {
    title 'gym.nygard.dev and gymbro.nygard.dev — the training log'
    description '
      Journey four, and the first one with a person in it who writes rather
      than reads. Two front doors open on the same room: the logger is a phone
      held in one hand with a bar in the other, the planner is the same block
      on a desk. Behind them there is one API, one Cosmos container and one
      Entra registration, and that is the whole design rather than a stage it
      has not grown out of.

      Both pages fetch from two places, and the split is the interesting part.
      The exercise library and the built-in day templates are identical for
      every account, so they are anonymous blobs in the same container the
      running dashboard is published to — no token, no function call, no RU,
      cached for a day. Everything a person wrote goes through the API with a
      bearer token instead, because it is theirs.

      The registration is drawn because it is load-bearing and because it is
      the one thing here no terraform apply can create. Easy Auth checks both
      that the token was minted for GymLog and that it was obtained by GymLog,
      so the planner signs in as the logger rather than as itself.
      `gymSignIn` is that exchange in order.

      Only the logger legs to Entra and to the blob are drawn. The planner
      makes exactly the same sign-in to the same registration and fetches
      exactly the same two files, and a second arrow for either crossed
      another to add nothing — the box each one points at says what it is.
      What the planner does differently is on the arrow it keeps: the same
      routes, a whole block at a time.

      What the API does with the token is the whole tenancy boundary: the
      Entra object id off the validated principal is the Cosmos partition key,
      and it is never read from a route, a query string or a body.
      `gymSet` is one write, and what a lost reply costs.
    '

    include
      user

    // Boxed together because they are one application in two shells, and
    // because it keeps them adjacent: two front ends reaching the same three
    // things is a fan that crosses itself unless they stay side by side.
    group 'The training log, in two shells' {
      include gym, gymbro
    }

    include
      entra,
      entra.gymlog,
      api,
      api.gymApi,
      cosmos,
      cdn,
      cdn.dataContainer

    exclude
      user -> api,             // the one-time WHOOP consent, on the same app — see `whoopBootstrap`
      api -> entra.gymlog,     // what Easy Auth checks, and in what order — see `gymSignIn`
      gymbro -> entra.gymlog,       // identical to the logger leg, and drawn twice it only adds crossings
      gymbro -> cdn.dataContainer   // the same two files the logger fetches; two fronts to two targets always cross once

    autoLayout LeftRight 150 110

    // Entra is not ours, the same way WHOOP is not ours on `running`. The
    // registration inside it is amber by kind because most app registrations
    // in this model are CI identities; this one is not.
    style entra, entra.gymlog {
      color muted
    }
  }

  dynamic view gymSignIn {
    title 'Signing in to the training log'
    description '
      How a page ends up holding a token the API will accept, and why there is
      only one registration in it.

      Nothing here has a client secret and nothing can: a browser cannot keep
      one, so the code exchange is PKCE and what makes the flow safe is that
      the redirect URI is registered and the verifier never left the page. The
      registration has to be on the Single-page application platform for that
      — the same URI registered under Web looks identical in the portal and
      fails at sign-in, because a Web redirect URI makes Entra treat the caller
      as a confidential client and demand the secret a browser cannot hold.

      Step seven is where the two checks that matter happen, and they are not
      the same check. One asks who the token was minted for; the other asks
      which client obtained it. A front end with a registration of its own
      passes the first and fails the second, which is exactly why the planner
      signs in as GymLog and why giving it an identity of its own is a
      terraform change rather than a front-end one.

      Step eight is the point of all of it. The object id off the validated
      principal is the Cosmos partition key, so it decides whose training log
      the call reads — which is why it is taken from the platform headers and
      never from anything the request could have said about itself.
    '

    user -> gym 'Taps sign in'
    gym -> entra.gymlog 'Redirects to Entra as GymLog: authorization code with PKCE, no secret'
    entra.gymlog -> gym 'Code back to the bridge page, which broadcasts it to the app'
    gym -> entra.gymlog 'Exchanges the code, then asks for an access token for the API scope'
    entra.gymlog -> gym 'An access token: minted for GymLog, obtained by GymLog'
    gym -> api 'Calls a gym route with the token in the Authorization header'
    api -> entra.gymlog 'Easy Auth validates it — signature, issuer tenant, audience, and which client obtained it'
    api -> cosmos 'The route reads the object id off the validated principal, and partitions on that'

    // Left to right, so the page and Entra sit side by side and the four legs
    // between them stay a straight bundle; top to bottom, the call to the API
    // had to loop round that bundle to get past Entra.
    autoLayout LeftRight

    // Grey, as on `gym`: this registration is Entra, not our delivery plane,
    // and the kind is amber because most app registrations in this model are
    // the identity a workflow runs as.
    style entra.gymlog {
      color muted
    }
  }

  dynamic view gymSet {
    title 'Logging a set, and what a lost reply costs'
    description '
      One tap, and then the same tap again after the reply went missing. This
      is the behaviour the whole front end is built on, so it is worth a page.

      Every write carries the number of sets the page believes the exercise
      already holds, and the patch applies only while that is still true.
      Steps one to four are the ordinary case. Five to seven are the same
      request arriving a second time — a reply lost on gym wifi, a tap
      repeated, an offline queue replayed on reconnect — and the count no
      longer matching is what makes it a no-op rather than a second set.

      Answering that with a 200 rather than an error is the deliberate part.
      "Already recorded" means the first attempt landed, so the client has
      nothing to reconcile and no rule to write about which of two replies to
      believe. That is what makes the one-tap button safe to hammer, and it is
      also what lets the page draw the row on the tap instead of three hundred
      milliseconds later: the count the server checks is what makes drawing
      first safe rather than optimistic.

      The other outcome is a refusal that says so — the page is stale, nothing
      was written, and the answer carries what the count actually is so the
      client re-reads the session and logs again from what it holds.
    '

    user -> gym 'Taps "Log same again"'
    gym -> api.gymApi 'POSTs the set, carrying the set count the page believes the exercise holds'
    api.gymApi -> cosmos 'Patches the session — but only while the stored count still matches'
    api.gymApi -> gym '200: recorded, and the row the page already drew is now stored'
    gym -> api.gymApi 'The reply never arrived, so the same request again'
    api.gymApi -> cosmos 'The count no longer matches, so the patch does not apply'
    api.gymApi -> gym '200 alreadyRecorded: the first attempt landed, and nothing was written twice'

    autoLayout TopBottom
  }
}
